Biography of Zhang Shi and Feng Tang

Zhang Tingyi Shi was a native of Du Yang, with the courtesy name Ji. He had an elder brother named Zhong who lived with him. He served as a cavalry officer under the command of Sima Zou, serving Emperor Xiaowen. For ten years he could not be transferred, and had no reputation. Shi said, "Long service has reduced my elder brother's produce; it will not do." He wished to excuse himself and return. The Left General Yuan Ang knew of his worth, pitied his departure, and therefore requested that Shi be transferred to replace the attendant. After Shi had finished his court audience, he took the opportunity to speak of what was expedient. Emperor Xiaowen said, "Keep it simple; do not speak too highly. Let it be something that can be put into practice now." Thereupon Shi spoke of the idle matters of Qin and Han, why Qin lost and why Han rose. Emperor Xiaowen approved, and appointed Shi as an attendant and archer.

Shi accompanied the emperor on a journey and entered the Tiger Circle. The emperor asked the Superintendent of the Upper Forest about the registers of birds and beasts, asking more than ten questions. The superintendent looked left and right and could not answer any. The Tiger Circle attendant Guo, from the side, substituted for the superintendent and answered the emperor's questions about the registers of birds and beasts in great detail, wishing to observe how his ability in oral response was inexhaustible. Emperor Xiaowen said, "Is it proper for an official to be like this? The superintendent is excused!" He then ordered Shi to appoint Guo as Superintendent of the Upper Forest. Shi said beforehand, "How does Your Majesty regard Lord Zhou Bo of Jing?" The emperor said, "He is a senior." Shi asked again, "How does Your Majesty regard Lord Zhang Xiang of Dongyang?" The emperor again said, "He is a senior." Shi said, "If Lord Zhou Bo and Lord Zhang Xiang are called seniors, these two men could not even bring their words out of their mouths. How much more this Guo, who is a sly, quick-tongued man who readily gives answers! Moreover, Qin employed officials with pens and brushes; officials competed to be diligent, quick, and meticulous in examining one another, yet their broken and useless writings were merely words, with no real substance. Therefore their faults were not heard, and it was delayed until the second generation, and the world fell into collapse. Now if Your Majesty promotes Guo because of his oral debate, I fear the world will follow the wind and be lax, competing for oral debate without substance. Moreover, subordinates are quick to respond to the ruler's appearance and sound; raising errors cannot but be investigated." Emperor Xiaowen said, "Good." He then stopped and did not appoint Guo.

The emperor mounted the carriage and summoned Shi to ride along. He proceeded slowly and asked Shi about the broken state of Qin. Shi answered with substantive words. When they arrived at the palace, the emperor appointed Shi as Superintendent of the Imperial Carriage.

Some time later, the crown prince and the Prince of Liang shared a carriage entering court, and did not dismount at the Gate of the Imperial Guards. Shi then stopped the crown prince and the Prince of Liang from entering the palace gate. He then impeached them for not dismounting at the public gate and not showing respect, and reported it. Empress Shangguan heard of it. Emperor Xiaowen removed his hat and thanked her, saying, "I taught my sons not to be careful." Empress Shangguan then sent envoys to accept the edict and pardon the crown prince and the Prince of Liang, and only then were they allowed to enter. From this, Emperor Xiaowen was surprised by Shi and appointed him as a Grand Warden.

Some time later, he became Left General. Accompanying the emperor to the Northern Tomb, he stayed at the north-facing toilet. At that time, Lady Shen accompanied them. The emperor instructed Lady Shen about the new abundance of the Dao and said, "This is the road to Handan." He made Lady Shen play the zither, and the emperor himself leaned on the zither and sang, his meaning one of sorrow and grief. He looked at the officials and said, "Alas! To use northern mountain stone as a burial mound, to use silk floss to wrap the body, to lacquer the coffin's interior—how can this be moved!" The attendants all said, "Good." Shi stepped forward and said, "If there is something desirable within, even if it is buried in the southern mountains there is still a grave; if there is nothing desirable within, even if there is no stone mound, what would one grieve over!" Emperor Xiaowen approved. Later he appointed Shi as Minister of Justice.

Some time later, the emperor traveled out over the Zhongwei Bridge. A man came out from under the bridge, and the carriage horses were startled. Shi then sent riders to capture him and handed him to the Minister of Justice. Shi questioned him. He said, "I am a county man who came to court. Hearing of the imperial procession, I hid under the bridge. After a long time, thinking the procession had passed, I came out, saw the carriage and horses and riders, and immediately ran." The Minister of Justice Qin should have fined one person for violating the procession. Emperor Xiaowen was angry and said, "This man personally startled my horses. My horses are gentle and docile; let them be other horses—how could they not injure me? Yet the Minister of Justice wants to fine him!" Shi said, "The law is what the Son of Heaven shares with the world. Now if the law is like this and is made heavier, then the law is not trustworthy to the people. Moreover, at the time, the emperor sent out the punishment, and it was already done. Now that it has come to the Minister of Justice, the Minister of Justice is the balance of the world; if he tilts it, then all the world's use of law becomes light or heavy. Where will the people place their hands and feet? It is for Your Majesty to observe." After a long time, the emperor said, "The Minister of Justice should be like this."

Later, someone stole a jade ring in front of the High Temple. He was captured, and Emperor Xiaowen was angry and sent the Minister of Justice to deal with it. Shi examined the statutes for stealing an ancestral temple's sacrificial vessels and reported that it should be abandoned and the market opened. The emperor was very angry and said, "The way of people is such that they steal the temple vessels of the previous emperor. We, the Ministers of Justice, wish to exterminate his clan, but you use the law to report it. This is not how we jointly receive the meaning of the ancestral temple." Shi removed his hat, lowered his head, and thanked him, saying, "The law is sufficient as it is. Moreover, the crimes are equal; only the difference of rebellion and obedience distinguishes them. Now to exterminate the clan for stealing ancestral temple vessels—there is not even one in ten million. Suppose an ignorant commoner takes a handful of soil from the Long Tomb; how would Your Majesty add to the law?" After a long time, Emperor Xiaowen spoke with the empress, and then allowed the Minister of Justice's judgment. At that time, the Chief Commandant Lord Zhou Yafu and the Prince of Liang Lord Wang Dui, the Marquis of Shandu, saw Shi upholding the discussion of peace, and became close friends. From this, Zhang the Minister of Justice was praised under heaven.

Later Emperor Xiaowen died, and Emperor Jing rose. Shi was afraid and claimed illness. He wished to avoid office, fearing great punishment; he wished to see the emperor to thank him, but did not know what would happen. He used Wang Sheng's plan and finally saw the emperor. Emperor Jing did not go beyond it.

Wang Sheng was good at speaking as an old man and was a scholar. He had once been invited to stay in the court, and the Three Excellencies and Nine Ministers all assembled. Wang Sheng, the old man, said, "I am puzzled," and looked at Zhang the Minister of Justice: "Help me solve the puzzle!" Shi knelt and solved it. After it was done, someone said to Wang Sheng, "Why would you alone humiliate Zhang the Minister of Justice and make him kneel to solve a puzzle?" Wang Sheng said, "I am old and lowly; I myself have no benefit to Zhang the Minister of Justice. Zhang the Minister of Justice is now a famous official of the world. I therefore slightly humiliated the Minister of Justice and made him kneel to solve the puzzle, wishing to elevate him." The officials heard this, and praised Wang Sheng for elevating Zhang the Minister of Justice.

Zhang the Minister of Justice served Emperor Jing for more than a year as the Chancellor of Huainan, still retaining his former faults. After a long time, Shi died. His son was named Zhang Zhi, with the courtesy name Zhanggong. He rose to the rank of Grand Warden and was released. Because he could not win favor in his time, he therefore never served for the rest of his life.

Feng Tang: his grandfather was Zhao Ren. His father was Shi Dai. In the rise of Han, he moved to Anling. Tang was filial and upright, and served as the head of the Secretariat under Emperor Xiaowen. When Emperor Xiaowen's palanquin passed by, he asked Tang, "Why did your father become a general? Where is your family from?" Tang answered him fully and truthfully. Emperor Xiaowen said, "When I was in Dai, I was still eating the food of the imperial kitchen, and several people spoke to me of the worth of General Zhao Liqi, who fought under the great deer. Now every time I eat, my thought has never been away from the great deer. Does your father know this?" Tang answered, "He is not as good as Lian Qiao or Li Mu as a general." The emperor said, "Why?" Tang said, "My grandfather served as an official under Zhao and was a general, and he admired Li Mu. My father therefore served as the Chancellor of Dai and admired General Zhao Liqi, and knew his character." After the emperor had heard of Lian Qiao and Li Mu's characters, he spoke well of them, and sighed, "Alas! I alone did not obtain Lian Qiao and Li Mu in their time to be my generals. Would I then worry about the Xiongnu!" Tang said, "Your Majesty, even if you obtained Lian Qiao and Li Mu, you could not use them." The emperor was angry and rose to go into the inner palace. After a long time, he summoned Tang and said, "How could you alone humiliate me? Is there no place for you to be at ease?" Tang thanked him and said, "I am a rustic and do not know what is taboo."

At that time, the Xiongnu newly entered court and killed the Northern Superintendent of the Army, Yang. The emperor took the Hu raid as a sign and again asked Tang, "How do you know I cannot use Lian Qiao and Li Mu?" Tang answered, "I have heard that in ancient times the kings who sent out generals knelt and pushed the carriage, saying: 'Those within the threshold are controlled by me, the commoner; those outside the threshold are controlled by the general.' Military merit and rewards are all decided outside, and then reported back. This is not an empty statement. My grandfather said that Li Mu, as Zhao's general, lived on the border; the military market's income was all used to feast the soldiers, and rewards were decided outside, not disturbing the center. He was entrusted with responsibility and held to success, and therefore Li Mu was able to exhaust his intelligence. He selected three thousand chariots, thirteen thousand cavalry, and one hundred thousand men of a hundred thousand cash, and thus drove the Single Rider north, broke the Eastern Hu, destroyed the Tancha Forest, and calmed the strong Qin in the west, and supported Han and Wei in the south. At that time, Zhao was nearly hegemonic. Later, when Zhao's king Xian was established, his mother was Cheng. When King Xian was established, he used Guo Kai's slander and finally punished Li Mu, ordering Yan Ju to replace him. Therefore the soldiers were broken in the north and were captured and destroyed by Qin. Now I secretly hear that Wei Shang is the Cloud Center Warden; his military market income is entirely used to feast soldiers and cavalry, and he privately keeps money, slaughtering one ox every five days, feasting guests, soldiers, and attendants. Therefore the Xiongnu keep far away and do not approach the Cloud Center's frontier. The captives once entered, and he still led chariots and cavalry to attack them, killing many of their number. Now soldiers and cavalry are all family men and sons; they rise from the fields and follow the army. How would they know the measure of their units and tallies? They fight hard all day, behead and capture captives, and report merit to the top. If one word does not correspond, the civil officials bind them with the law. If rewards do not go out, the officials, upholding the law, must use them. I am foolish and think Your Majesty's law is too clear, rewards too light, and punishments too heavy. Moreover, the Cloud Center Warden Wei Shang sits at the top with the merit of capturing a leader of six ranks; Your Majesty demotes his officials, strips their ranks, and makes them work. From this, Your Majesty, even if you obtained Lian Qiao and Li Mu, you could not use them. I am truly foolish, I have touched the taboo, and deserve death, death!" Emperor Xiaowen was persuaded. That day he ordered Feng Tang to take the tally and pardon Wei Shang, restore him to Cloud Center Warden, and appointed Tang as Colonel of Cavalry, Chief Commandant, and the head of the chariot officers of commanderies and kingdoms.

Seven years later, Emperor Jing rose and appointed Tang as Chancellor of Chu, and he was released. Emperor Wu rose and sought the worthy and good, and recommended Feng Tang. Tang was then over ninety years old and could not again serve as an official, so he recommended his son Feng Sui as a general. Sui had the courtesy name Wangsun, and was also a remarkable man, and was good with the rest.

The Grand Historian says: Zhang Ji's words about the senior, upholding the law and not yielding to intention; Feng Gong's discussion of leading generals—how it has flavor! How it has flavor! It is said, "Not knowing the man, look at his friends." What the two emperors recited and studied can be written on the gallery and temple. The book says, "Not partial and not factional, the king's way is vast; not factional and not partial, the king's way is easy." Zhang Ji and Feng Gong are near it. [end of text]


